    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **262 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented rejection of a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400 response.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`src/main/java/.../v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n\n```java\nif (ownerFieldsDto.getRegistrationDate() != null\n    && ownerFieldsDto.getRegistrationDate().isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);\n}\n```\n\nThis runs before the existing default-to-`LocalDate.now()` logic. It only rejects an *explicitly supplied* future date; a `null` registrationDate still defaults to the server's current date, and today's date is accepted (`isAfter` is strict).\n\n**Tests added** (`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`):\n- `createOwnerRejectedWhenRegistrationDateInFuture` \u2014 tomorrow's date \u2192 400.\n- `createOwnerAllowedWhenRegistrationDateToday` \u2014 today's date \u2192 201, confirming the boundary isn't over-rejected.\n\nI placed the guard in the create path only, matching the spec (registration date is a create-time field \u2014 `updateOwner` never touches `registrationDate`).",
